index 0000000..c035a22
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/self_hosting.md
@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+## Self-hosting Fizzy on one box with Kamal
+
+Run your own Fizzy on a single Linux server (Docker, SQLite, one container,
+HTTPS via kamal-proxy). Fork the repo, fill in three things, `kamal setup`.
+
+### What this is
+
+* **One box**: a single server from `config/deploy.yml` (`servers.web`).
+  No separate job host, no MySQL/Redis — SQLite + Solid Queue/Cache/Cable.
+* **Everything in one container**: the web container also runs background jobs
+  via the Solid Queue Puma plugin (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=true`). See
+  `config/puma.rb`.
+* **SSL from the proxy**: `proxy.ssl: true` in `config/deploy.yml` provisions a
+  Let's Encrypt certificate for your host automatically.
+* **Data survives redeploys**: SQLite databases (`storage/*.sqlite3`) and
+  uploads (`storage/production/files`) live under `/rails/storage`, persisted in
+  the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume (`volumes:` in `config/deploy.yml`).
+  `bin/docker-entrypoint` runs `db:prepare` on boot, so the first deploy
+  creates the schema and later deploys just migrate.
+
+This is separate from 37signals' SaaS deploys (`saas/config/deploy.*.yml`,
+MySQL, multi-host, `-d <destination>`). Don't mix them: self-host uses plain
+`bin/kamal` (defaults to `config/deploy.yml`), SaaS uses `bin/kamal -d ...`
+in SaaS mode.
+
+### Prerequisites
+
+* A Linux server (x86_64) with SSH access and ports 80/443 open. DNS for your
+  hostname must point at it (needed for Let's Encrypt).
+* Docker Hub account (or another registry) for the built image.
+* An SMTP account for outgoing mail (magic-link codes, notifications). Any
+  provider works: Postmark, SendGrid, SES, your own Postfix, etc.
+* Kamal runs from your laptop: Ruby + `bundle install` (includes the `kamal`
+  gem), and `docker` login-capable.
+
+### 1. Fill in your hostname
+
+In `config/deploy.yml` replace the placeholders:
+
+| Placeholder | Replace with |
+|---|---|
+| `YOUR_SERVER_IP_OR_HOSTNAME` | your server's IP or SSH hostname |
+| `YOUR_HOST.example.com` (proxy `host`, `APP_HOST`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`, `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`) | your public FQDN, e.g. `fizzy.example.com` |
+| `your-dockerhub-user/fizzy`, `your-dockerhub-username` | your image repo + registry username |
+
+`APP_HOST` must match `proxy.host` — Rails uses it for links inside emails
+(`config/environments/production.rb`).
+
+### 2. Fill in your mail server
+
+Under `env.clear` in `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+```yaml
+SMTP_ADDRESS: smtp.your-provider.com
+SMTP_PORT: 587
+SMTP_DOMAIN: fizzy.example.com
+SMTP_USERNAME: your-smtp-username
+```
+
+Password/token goes in secrets (next step) as `SMTP_PASSWORD`. Optional knobs
+(read in `config/environments/production.rb`): `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`
+(defaults to `plain` when a username is set), `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO`
+(default `true`). Without an SMTP server, delivery falls back to
+`localhost:25`; set a real server so login codes arrive.
+
+### 3. Fill in your secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` is git-safe (references only). Export in your shell:
+
+```sh
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD="your-docker-hub-access-token"  # registry password
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE="$(openssl rand -hex 64)"                # Rails secret
+export SMTP_PASSWORD="your-mail-provider-password-or-token"
+# Only if you use Rails credentials:
+# export RAILS_MASTER_KEY="$(cat config/master.key)"
+```
+
+Generate a fresh `SECRET_KEY_BASE` per installation and keep it — changing it
+invalidates sessions.
+
+### 4. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup    # provisions Docker, proxy, volume; builds, pushes, boots
+bin/kamal deploy   # later deploys (data stays in the fizzy_storage volume)
+```
+
+Then open `https://YOUR_HOST`. Sign in with your email; the code arrives via
+your SMTP server.
+
+### Operations
+
+* Logs: `bin/kamal app logs`
+* Console: `bin/kamal app exec --interactive --reuse "bin/rails console"`
+* Data: `bin/kamal app exec --interactive --reuse "sqlite3 storage/production.sqlite3 .tables"`
+* Back up the box with `docker volume inspect fizzy_storage` / your host
+  snapshots — everything stateful is in that one volume.
+
+### Why SQLite survives
+
+`config/database.sqlite.yml` (production) keeps `primary`, `cable`, `cache`,
+and `queue` databases as files under `storage/`. The Kamal `volumes:` entry
+mounts a named volume over `/rails/storage`, so replacing the container keeps
+the files. Removing the volume is the only thing that deletes data.
